From c48c111f9865236a470b272131512115347ad6c9 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Thu, 6 Aug 2026 14:28:41 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Build the raw arm for the tree root, and refute the widening
 on it

0109 found the widening collected 868 of the 18.7 M boxes it was
selected to remove, because item 3-8a's representation had raw arms for
the tree's leaf, the element read and the element write and none for its
ROOT -- the one site the saving lives at. This is that arm: a guarded
tree whose destination is a speculative local writes the raw half and
the flag itself, on the guarded arm, and the slot and a lowered flag on
the other. No box on either. Statement position only, the line
NumericStoreResult already draws, wired at the assignment and at the
declarator.

It works: roots consumed by a refused local go 18 657 804 -> 16 225 570,
0.870, so 2 431 366 boxes are removed against 0109's 868.

And the item is refuted anyway. Against the widening-off arm the corpus
is still 1.041 on boxing requests and 1.039 on allocations, because the
cost did not move at all: speculative-read boxes are 7 692 133 in both
arms. The saving is 2.4 M and the cost is 7.7 M.

Two things this settles that no earlier count could.

0108's consumer-side bound was 25x too low, and structurally so. It
counted reads at five consumer positions; the cost is a box at EVERY
read of the speculative local that is not one of the three raw-capable
consumers, minted at the local's own read expression -- precisely the
site with no safe hook. A lower bound taken at the wrong sites is not a
loose bound, it is a bound on a different quantity.

And the saving was never 14.2 M. A refusal census attributes a name to
its FIRST cause, so removing that cause admits the name only if it was
the ONLY blocker. `var t = a[0] * b + i` with a parameter b is charged
to ElementRead and still refused once elements are assumed numeric.
That is why 6.9 M of ElementRead writes yield 2.4 M.

Off by default and staying off. 8 173 tests with the switch off and
8 064 across five projects with it forced on, no failures either way.
